"""Master Data File for NCERT Class 5 Mathematics Chapter 2: Fractions."""

CHAPTER_META = {'title': 'Fractions', 'class': 'Class 5', 'subject': 'Mathematics', 'chapter': 2, 'book': 'Joyful Mathematics', 'total_slides': 20, 'concept_slides': 16, 'quiz_slides': 4, 'total_checkpoints': 32, 'total_quiz_questions': 40}

SLIDES = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Equal Wholes', 'subtitle': 'Why Comparing Fractions Requires Identical Wholes (Chocolates & Parathas)', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">A fraction represents a part of a whole. But here is the fundamental rule of fractions: <strong>to compare two fractions fairly, the whole items from which they are taken must be identical in size!</strong></p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Paratha Dilemma</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">If Kabir eats 1/2 of a small baby paratha and Ananya eats 1/2 of a large family paratha, do they eat the same amount? <strong>No!</strong> Ananya eats much more, even though both fractions say 1/2, because the whole parathas were different sizes!</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Chocolate Bar Sharing</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">When two friends share from the <em>same</em> identical chocolate bar, 1/4 of that bar is truly equal for both, and 1/2 is always bigger than 1/4.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Always remember: a fraction is a relative comparison. Always check the size of the whole before comparing fractional shares!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Welcome young mathematicians to the wonderful world of fractions! Every single day in our homes, we share food: we break a chocolate bar into equal squares, slice a round paratha for lunch, or pour half a glass of milk. A fraction simply tells us how many equal parts we take out of a whole. But before we begin calculating, there is one golden rule you must lock in your memory: to compare any two fractions, the wholes they come from must be completely identical in size! Imagine two friends having lunch. One friend eats half of a tiny baby paratha, while another friend eats half of a giant family-sized paratha. Both say they ate one-half, but did they eat the same amount of food? Certainly not! Half of a giant paratha is much larger than half of a tiny one. Fractions are relative to their whole. Only when the original whole shapes are identical can we say that one-half is greater than one-fourth. Look at the interactive paratha comparison simulator on your screen, switch between different whole sizes, and see why identical wholes are the bedrock of mathematical fairness!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Rohit eats 1/2 of a small chocolate bar and Priya eats 1/2 of a mega chocolate bar. Did they eat the same amount of chocolate?', 'options': ['Yes, because both ate 1/2', "No, because Priya's whole chocolate bar was larger", "No, because Rohit's chocolate was darker", 'Yes, fractions always mean equal quantities'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Fractions can only be compared directly when the wholes are identical. 1/2 of a larger chocolate is more chocolate than 1/2 of a smaller bar.', 'study_insight': 'A fraction is always a portion of a specific whole; if the wholes differ, the amounts differ.'}, {'question': 'When can we strictly say that 3/4 of a pizza is greater than 1/2 of a pizza?', 'options': ['Only when the two pizzas are identical in size', 'Whenever we like, regardless of pizza sizes', 'Only if the pizza has round slices', 'Only when eating in a restaurant'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'To compare fractions mathematically, both fractions must refer to the exact same whole or identical wholes.', 'study_insight': 'Fairness and accuracy in fractions always begin with identical reference wholes.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Grid Shading', 'subtitle': 'Visualizing Fractions on Square Grids (Grids A, B, and C)', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Square grids are wonderful geometric tools for visualizing fractions. When a square grid is divided into equal smaller squares, each tiny square is a unit fraction of the whole grid.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-2 my-3 text-center">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-red-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-red-400 block mb-1">Grid A (16 Squares)</span>\n                    <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">Shade <strong>1/8</strong> in Red:<br>1/8 of 16 = <strong>2 squares</strong></p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-blue-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-400 block mb-1">Grid B (24 Squares)</span>\n                    <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">Shade <strong>1/6</strong> in Blue:<br>1/6 of 24 = <strong>4 squares</strong></p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-amber-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Grid C (36 Squares)</span>\n                    <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">Shade <strong>1/12</strong> in Yellow:<br>1/12 of 36 = <strong>3 squares</strong></p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">To find a fraction of a grid: multiply the total number of squares by the numerator, then divide by the denominator. Notice how different patterns can represent the same fraction!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us play with square grids! Square grids allow us to see fractions with our own eyes. Imagine you have a square sheet divided into sixteen identical smaller squares, which we call Grid A. If you want to color one-eighth of this grid in red, how many squares should you color? We divide the total sixteen squares by eight, which gives two squares! So coloring any two squares out of sixteen gives exactly one-eighth. Next, take Grid B, which has twenty-four equal squares. To shade one-sixth in blue, we divide twenty-four by six, which gives four squares. And on Grid C with thirty-six squares, shading one-twelfth in bright yellow requires thirty-six divided by twelve, which equals three squares! What makes grid shading truly fascinating is that it does not matter which specific squares you shade: they can be in a straight line, grouped in a block, or scattered across the grid. As long as the count of shaded squares is correct, the total fractional area remains identical! Use the interactive grid canvas on your screen to click and shade squares, and watch the fraction counter update!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A square grid has 24 equal squares. How many squares must you shade to cover 1/3 of the grid?', 'options': ['6 squares', '8 squares', '12 squares', '4 squares'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'To find 1/3 of 24 squares, divide 24 by 3: 24 ÷ 3 = 8 squares.', 'study_insight': '1/3 of any total is found by dividing the total into 3 equal groups.'}, {'question': 'If 6 squares out of 18 equal squares in a grid are shaded green, what fraction of the grid is shaded?', 'options': ['1/2', '1/3', '1/6', '1/4'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '6 out of 18 is 6/18. Dividing numerator and denominator by 6 gives 1/3.', 'study_insight': 'Grouping 18 squares into 3 equal sets of 6 proves that 6/18 equals 1/3.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Fraction Kit', 'subtitle': 'Exploring Physical Fraction Strips and Building Wholes', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">A <strong>Fraction Kit</strong> consists of colorful rectangular strips of identical total length, each cut into different equal fractional pieces: 1 (whole), 1/2 (halves), 1/4 (fourths), 1/8 (eighths), and 1/16 (sixteenths).</p>\n            <div class="space-y-2 my-3 font-mono text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2 rounded bg-amber-500/20 border border-amber-500/40 text-amber-300 text-center font-bold">1 Whole Strip</div>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-1 text-center">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 rounded bg-blue-500/20 border border-blue-500/40 text-blue-300 font-bold">1/2</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 rounded bg-blue-500/20 border border-blue-500/40 text-blue-300 font-bold">1/2</div>\n                </div>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-4 gap-1 text-center">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/20 border border-emerald-500/40 text-emerald-300 font-bold">1/4</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/20 border border-emerald-500/40 text-emerald-300 font-bold">1/4</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/20 border border-emerald-500/40 text-emerald-300 font-bold">1/4</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 rounded bg-emerald-500/20 border border-emerald-500/40 text-emerald-300 font-bold">1/4</div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">By aligning pieces side-by-side, students physically experience how smaller fractional parts combine to match the length of larger pieces and recreate the complete whole strip.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'In your mathematics classroom, one of the most exciting tools you will use is the Fraction Kit! Imagine taking long, colorful strips of paper that all have the exact same length. The first strip is left whole, colored in warm amber, representing the number one. The second strip is folded precisely in the middle and cut into two equal pieces, giving two blue pieces labeled one-half. The third strip is folded in half and in half again, creating four equal green pieces labeled one-fourth. And if you fold further, you get eight purple pieces labeled one-eighth! When you place these strips next to each other on your desk, mathematical relationships become crystal clear. You can physically see that two one-half pieces stretch out to match the full length of the whole strip. Four one-fourth pieces fit snugly together to make one whole, and eight one-eighth pieces do the exact same thing! The fraction kit transforms abstract numbers into touchable, visual building blocks. Use the interactive strip builder on your screen to drag and snap pieces together, and watch how fractions combine to create perfect wholes!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many 1/4 strips from the fraction kit are needed to equal the length of one 1/2 strip?', 'options': ['1 piece', '2 pieces', '4 pieces', '3 pieces'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '1/4 + 1/4 = 2/4 = 1/2. Exactly two 1/4 pieces match the length of one 1/2 piece.', 'study_insight': 'Because 4 is twice 2, each fourth is half the size of a half, so two fourths make one half.'}, {'question': 'How many 1/8 pieces are needed to make one complete whole strip?', 'options': ['4 pieces', '6 pieces', '8 pieces', '16 pieces'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The denominator 8 means the whole is divided into 8 equal parts. So 8 pieces of 1/8 make 8/8 = 1 whole.', 'study_insight': 'The denominator tells you exactly how many equal parts make the whole.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Piece Relations', 'subtitle': 'Discovering Equivalence and Part-to-Part Proportions', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">By comparing different pieces from our Fraction Kit, we uncover precise mathematical relationships connecting halves, fourths, eighths, and sixteenths.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Fitting Halves and Fourths</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">• Two 1/4 pieces = <strong>1/2</strong> (2/4 = 1/2)<br>• Four 1/4 pieces = <strong>1 Whole</strong> (4/4 = 1)<br>• Four 1/8 pieces = <strong>1/2</strong> (4/8 = 1/2)</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-cyan-400 block mb-1">Fitting Eighths and Sixteenths</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">• Two 1/8 pieces = <strong>1/4</strong> (2/8 = 1/4)<br>• Two 1/16 pieces = <strong>1/8</strong> (2/16 = 1/8)<br>• Eight 1/8 pieces = <strong>1 Whole</strong> (8/8 = 1)</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Notice the pattern: as the denominator doubles, the size of each piece is halved. Therefore, you need twice as many pieces to cover the same length!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us examine how the different pieces of our fraction kit relate to one another like members of a mathematical family! When you lay down a one-half strip, take two one-fourth pieces and place them directly on top. They match edge to edge! This proves visually that two-fourths is exactly equal to one-half. Now, take one-eighth pieces. How many one-eighth pieces do you think will cover one-fourth? Two one-eighth pieces cover one-fourth perfectly! And how many one-eighth pieces cover one-half? Exactly four one-eighth pieces! Do you see the marvelous pattern unfolding here? Whenever the denominator doubles—from two to four, or four to eight—the size of each individual piece becomes half as wide. Because each piece is cut in half, you need twice as many pieces to cover the exact same distance! That is why two-fourths, four-eighths, and eight-sixteenths all represent the exact same half of the strip. Use the interactive comparison bench on your screen to snap pieces together and watch how smaller parts combine into larger equivalents!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If you have three 1/4 pieces and two 1/8 pieces, what total fraction of the whole strip do you have?', 'options': ['3/4', '7/8', '1 Whole', '5/8'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Two 1/8 pieces equal 1/4 (2/8 = 1/4). So three 1/4 pieces + one 1/4 piece = four 1/4 pieces = 4/4 = 1 Whole!', 'study_insight': 'Converting 2/8 to 1/4 makes addition of like parts effortless.'}, {'question': 'How many 1/8 pieces are needed to match the length of three 1/4 pieces?', 'options': ['3 pieces', '4 pieces', '6 pieces', '8 pieces'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Each 1/4 piece equals two 1/8 pieces. So three 1/4 pieces = 3 × 2 = six 1/8 pieces (6/8 = 3/4).', 'study_insight': 'Multiplying the numerator and denominator by 2 reveals that 3/4 = 6/8.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Building Wholes', 'subtitle': 'Combining Unlike Fraction Pieces to Reconstruct the Whole', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In our textbook exercises, we practice puzzle grouping to build a complete whole using creative mixtures of different fraction pieces, such as combining thirds, sixths, and twelfths, or halves and fourths.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-2">Sample Whole-Building Recipes</span>\n                <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside">\n                    <li><strong>Recipe 1:</strong> 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4 = <strong>1 Whole</strong></li>\n                    <li><strong>Recipe 2:</strong> 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 + 1/8 = <strong>1 Whole</strong></li>\n                    <li><strong>Recipe 3:</strong> 1/3 + 2/6 + 4/12 = 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = <strong>1 Whole</strong></li>\n                    <li><strong>Recipe 4:</strong> 1/2 + 3/6 = 1/2 + 1/2 = <strong>1 Whole</strong></li>\n                </ul>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Just like preparing a recipe in the kitchen with half cups and quarter spoons, different fractional portions sum together harmoniously to form complete units.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Building a complete whole using a combination of different fraction pieces is just like solving a jigsaw puzzle! You do not always have to use pieces of the same color. Suppose you have one blue strip of one-half. To complete the whole, you still need another half. But what if you do not have another one-half piece? You can use two green one-fourth pieces instead! One-half plus one-fourth plus one-fourth gives one complete whole. What if you only have one one-fourth piece and some eighths? You can take one-half, add one-fourth, and then add two one-eighth pieces! One-half plus one-fourth plus two-eighths equals one whole. We can even do this with thirds, sixths, and twelfths. One-third plus two-sixths plus four-twelfths is really one-third plus one-third plus one-third, which equals three-thirds, or one whole! In daily life, a chef in the kitchen often combines a half-cup of milk, a quarter-cup of curd, and two one-eighth cups of water to fill a measuring jar. Test your puzzle skills on the interactive whole builder on your screen: select different piece combinations and verify if they fill the whole bar completely!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following combinations of fraction pieces equals exactly 1 Whole?', 'options': ['1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8', '1/2 + 1/4 + 2/8', '1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4', '1/2 + 1/3'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '2/8 = 1/4. So 1/2 + 1/4 + 2/8 = 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 Whole.', 'study_insight': 'Simplify smaller pieces to like denominators to quickly check if they reach 1.'}, {'question': 'If you already have 2/3 of a cake, what fraction piece must you add to make 1 Whole cake?', 'options': ['1/2', '1/3', '1/4', '2/6'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '1 Whole = 3/3. 3/3 - 2/3 = 1/3. Note that 2/6 is also equivalent to 1/3, but 1/3 is the direct unit piece.', 'study_insight': '3 parts out of 3 make the whole, so 2 parts need 1 more part.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Equivalent Fractions', 'subtitle': 'Visualizing Different Names for the Exact Same Shaded Region', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Imagine a chocolate bar divided into 3 equal columns. If you shade 1 column, you have shaded <strong>1/3</strong>. Now draw a horizontal cut dividing the bar into 2 equal rows: you now have 6 smaller rectangles, and 2 are shaded! The shaded region has not changed at all, but its name is now <strong>2/6</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3 text-center">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-2">The Equivalence Chain</span>\n                <div class="flex items-center justify-center space-x-2 font-mono text-sm text-amber-300 font-bold">\n                    <span>1/3</span>\n                    <span>=</span>\n                    <span>2/6</span>\n                    <span>=</span>\n                    <span>3/9</span>\n                    <span>=</span>\n                    <span>4/12</span>\n                    <span>=</span>\n                    <span>5/15</span>\n                </div>\n                <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 mt-2">Rule: Multiply or divide both numerator and denominator by the <em>same non-zero number</em> to generate equivalent fractions.</p>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Equivalent fractions look different because they have different numbers, but they represent the <strong>exact same value and area</strong>.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Let us explore one of the most powerful concepts in all of mathematics: Equivalent Fractions! The word equivalent comes from equal value. Equivalent fractions are simply different names for the exact same amount! Picture a chocolate bar divided into three long vertical strips. If you eat one strip, you have eaten one-third of the chocolate. Now, imagine taking a knife and cutting the entire chocolate bar horizontally across the middle. Each of the three strips is sliced into two pieces, making six pieces in total. How many pieces do you have now? You have two pieces out of six! Notice that the amount of chocolate on your plate has not changed by even a single crumb! You still have the exact same chocolate, but now its name is two-sixths. If we cut the original bar into three rows, we get three-ninths. If we cut it into four rows, we get four-twelfths! All of these—one-third, two-sixths, three-ninths, and four-twelfths—are equivalent fractions. Mathematically, whenever you multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the same number, you are simply cutting the parts into smaller pieces without changing the total amount! Use the horizontal cutter simulator on your screen to slice rectangles and watch equivalent fractions appear!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which fraction is equivalent to 1/3?', 'options': ['2/5', '3/9', '2/8', '3/6'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Multiplying numerator and denominator of 1/3 by 3 gives (1 × 3) / (3 × 3) = 3/9.', 'study_insight': 'Multiplying top and bottom by the same number preserves the fractional value.'}, {'question': 'If you multiply both the numerator and denominator of 3/5 by 4, what equivalent fraction do you get?', 'options': ['12/20', '7/9', '12/15', '9/20'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': '(3 × 4) / (5 × 4) = 12/20. Both 3/5 and 12/20 represent the exact same portion.', 'study_insight': 'Multiplying numerator and denominator by 4 divides each fifth into 4 smaller twentieths.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Equal Names', 'subtitle': 'Finding Missing Numerators and Denominators in Equivalence Equations', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To find a missing numerator or denominator in an equivalent fraction statement, find the multiplier connecting the known terms and apply it to the other term.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Example 1: 2/5 = ?/10</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Look at the denominators: from 5 to 10, we multiplied by 2 (5 × 2 = 10).<br>\n                    Therefore, multiply the numerator by 2 as well:<br>\n                    2 × 2 = <strong>4</strong>.<br>\n                    So, <strong>2/5 = 4/10</strong>!</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Example 2: 3/4 = 9/?</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Look at the numerators: from 3 to 9, we multiplied by 3 (3 × 3 = 9).<br>\n                    Therefore, multiply the denominator by 3 as well:<br>\n                    4 × 3 = <strong>12</strong>.<br>\n                    So, <strong>3/4 = 9/12</strong>!</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Cross-multiplication check: in any true equivalent fraction pair a/b = c/d, the cross products are equal: a × d = b × c!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us master the skill of solving equivalent fraction puzzles by finding missing numerators and denominators! Think of an equivalence statement as a balanced scale: whatever operation happens to the denominator must also happen to the numerator. Suppose you are given the puzzle: two-fifths equals box over ten. How do you find the missing number in the box? First, look at the known pair: the denominators five and ten. How did five become ten? We multiplied five by two! To keep the fraction balanced and equivalent, we must do the exact same thing to the numerator: multiply two by two. Two multiplied by two is four! Therefore, the missing numerator is four, and two-fifths equals four-tenths! Now consider another puzzle: three-fourths equals nine over box. Here, we look at the numerators: three became nine by multiplying by three. So we multiply the denominator four by three, which gives twelve! Three-fourths equals nine-twelfths. You can always check your answer using cross-multiplication: three multiplied by twelve is thirty-six, and four multiplied by nine is thirty-six! Test your skills on the interactive equivalence balance on your screen by filling in the missing boxes!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Find the missing numerator: 3/7 = ?/21', 'options': ['6', '9', '12', '15'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'From 7 to 21, we multiplied by 3 (7 × 3 = 21). So multiply the numerator by 3: 3 × 3 = 9. Thus 3/7 = 9/21.', 'study_insight': 'Whatever scale factor is applied to the denominator must be applied to the numerator.'}, {'question': 'Find the missing denominator: 4/5 = 16/?', 'options': ['15', '20', '25', '18'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'From 4 to 16, we multiplied by 4 (4 × 4 = 16). So multiply the denominator by 4: 5 × 4 = 20. Thus 4/5 = 16/20.', 'study_insight': 'Checking with cross multiplication: 4 × 20 = 80, and 5 × 16 = 80.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Same Denominator', 'subtitle': 'Comparing Fractions When Part Sizes are Equal (Chikki Slices)', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When two fractions have the <strong>same denominator</strong>, it means the whole has been divided into parts of the <strong>exact same size</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Comparing 1/3 and 2/3 of a Chikki</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Imagine a crunchy peanut chikki divided into 3 equal pieces. Each piece is 1/3.<br>\n                • Aarav gets 1 piece = <strong>1/3</strong><br>\n                • Meera gets 2 pieces = <strong>2/3</strong><br>\n                Since each piece is identical in size, having 2 pieces is clearly more than having 1 piece!<br>\n                Therefore: <strong>2/3 > 1/3</strong> (or <strong>1/3 < 2/3</strong>).</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 text-xs text-emerald-300">\n                <strong>Golden Rule for Same Denominator:</strong> Simply compare the numerators! The fraction with the greater numerator is the larger fraction. (e.g., 5/8 > 3/8 because 5 > 3).\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us learn how to compare fractions! The easiest comparison in fractions happens when both fractions share the exact same denominator. Remember what the denominator tells us: it tells us how many equal parts the whole is divided into. So if two fractions have the same denominator, every single piece in both fractions has the exact same size! Picture a rectangular peanut chikki bar broken into three equal pieces. Each piece is one-third of the chikki. If your friend takes one piece, they have one-third. If you take two pieces, you have two-thirds. Who has more chikki? You do, of course! Two pieces of the same size are naturally more than one piece. Therefore, two-thirds is greater than one-third. The golden rule is delightfully simple: when denominators are identical, just look at the numerators! The fraction with the larger numerator is always greater. For example, five-eighths is greater than three-eighths because five pieces are more than three pieces. Seven-tenths is greater than four-tenths. Use the chikki sharing simulator on your screen to adjust the numerators and observe how inequality signs change with the number of slices!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which sign correctly compares 5/7 and 3/7?', 'options': ['5/7 < 3/7', '5/7 > 3/7', '5/7 = 3/7', 'Cannot be determined'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Both fractions have the same denominator (7). Comparing numerators: 5 > 3, so 5/7 > 3/7.', 'study_insight': 'With like denominators, more parts mean a larger total portion.'}, {'question': 'Arrange in increasing (ascending) order: 5/9, 2/9, 7/9, 1/9.', 'options': ['7/9, 5/9, 2/9, 1/9', '1/9, 2/9, 5/9, 7/9', '2/9, 1/9, 5/9, 7/9', '1/9, 5/9, 2/9, 7/9'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Since all denominators are 9, we sort by numerators: 1 < 2 < 5 < 7. So: 1/9 < 2/9 < 5/9 < 7/9.', 'study_insight': 'When denominators match, ascending order corresponds directly to ascending numerators.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Same Numerator', 'subtitle': 'Comparing Fractions When Part Counts are Equal but Sizes Differ', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">What happens when two fractions have the <strong>same numerator</strong> but <strong>different denominators</strong>, such as comparing <strong>4/6</strong> and <strong>4/5</strong> of a paratha?</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Part Size Comparison</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A paratha cut into 5 equal pieces has larger slices than a paratha cut into 6 equal pieces.<br>\n                    Therefore, <strong>1/5 piece > 1/6 piece</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Taking 4 Pieces of Each</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Taking 4 pieces of the smaller size (4 × 1/6) is less than taking 4 pieces of the larger size (4 × 1/5).<br>\n                    Therefore: <strong>4/6 < 4/5</strong> (or <strong>4/5 > 4/6</strong>)!</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-indigo-950/40 border border-indigo-500/30 text-xs text-indigo-300">\n                <strong>Golden Rule for Same Numerator:</strong> The fraction with the <em>smaller denominator</em> has larger individual pieces, making it the <strong>larger fraction</strong>! (e.g., 3/4 > 3/8 because fourths are bigger than eighths).\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now here is a fascinating question that surprises many students: what happens when two fractions have the exact same numerator, but different denominators? For example, which is bigger: four-sixths of a paratha, or four-fifths of an identical paratha? Let us think carefully about the meaning of the denominator. If you divide a round paratha among five friends, each piece is one-fifth. If you divide that same paratha among six friends, you have to cut it into more slices, so each slice becomes smaller! This means one-fifth is distinctly larger than one-sixth. Now, both fractions have the numerator four, meaning you are taking four slices of each! Taking four large slices of one-fifth gives you much more food than taking four smaller slices of one-sixth. Therefore, four-fifths is greater than four-sixths! Notice the surprising rule: when numerators are the same, the fraction with the smaller denominator is actually the larger fraction! Why? Because fewer cuts make bigger pieces! Three-fourths is larger than three-eighths. Two-thirds is larger than two-fifths. Use the interactive paratha slice comparator on your screen to toggle slice sizes and see why smaller denominators give larger shares!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which fraction is larger: 3/5 or 3/7?', 'options': ['3/7, because 7 is greater than 5', '3/5, because fifths are larger pieces than sevenths', 'Both are equal because both numerators are 3', 'Cannot be determined without a calculator'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'When numerators are equal, the fraction with the smaller denominator has larger pieces. Since 5 < 7, 1/5 > 1/7, so 3/5 > 3/7.', 'study_insight': 'Fewer cuts mean bigger slices: fifths are bigger than sevenths.'}, {'question': 'Insert the correct symbol: 5/8 ___ 5/6', 'options': ['>', '<', '=', '+'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Since eighths are smaller than sixths (1/8 < 1/6), 5 eighths is less than 5 sixths: 5/8 < 5/6.', 'study_insight': 'A larger denominator with the same numerator indicates a smaller overall value.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Greater Than One', 'subtitle': 'Discovering Fractions Beyond One Whole (5/2, 9/4, and Sharing Parathas)', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Can a fraction be larger than 1 whole? <strong>Yes!</strong> In everyday life, when we share multiple items, we frequently encounter quantities greater than 1.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Sharing 5 Halves of Paratha</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Suppose you have 5 half-parathas (5 × 1/2). That is <strong>5/2</strong>.<br>\n                    • Two halves make 1 whole paratha.<br>\n                    • Another two halves make a 2nd whole paratha.<br>\n                    • 1 half is left over.<br>\n                    So 5/2 = <strong>2 whole parathas and 1/2</strong>!</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Improper Fractions</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">When the <strong>numerator is greater than or equal to the denominator</strong> (like 5/2, 9/4, 7/3), it is called an <strong>improper fraction</strong>, and its value is <strong>greater than or equal to 1</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Fractions where the numerator is smaller than the denominator (like 1/2, 3/4) are called proper fractions, and they are always less than 1.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Up until now, we have mostly looked at fractions that are smaller than one whole, like half a glass of juice or a quarter of a pizza. But what happens when you have more than one whole? Suppose your family makes fresh, hot parathas for breakfast. Each paratha is cut into two equal halves. If you eat five of these half-slices, what fraction have you eaten? You have eaten five halves, written as five over two! Notice that the top number, five, is greater than the bottom number, two. Whenever the numerator is larger than the denominator, we call it an improper fraction, and its value is strictly greater than one! Think about those five half-slices: the first two halves join together to make one whole paratha. The next two halves make a second whole paratha. And you still have one half-slice left! So eating five-halves is the exact same as eating two whole parathas plus one-half paratha. That is two and a half! Similarly, if you have nine quarter-slices of pizza, nine over four gives two whole pizzas and one-fourth left over. Use the paratha assembly simulator on your screen to bundle slices together and see how fractions naturally extend beyond one whole!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following is a fraction greater than 1 (an improper fraction)?', 'options': ['3/4', '5/6', '7/4', '2/3'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'In 7/4, the numerator 7 is greater than the denominator 4. 7/4 = 1 Whole and 3/4, which is greater than 1.', 'study_insight': 'Whenever numerator > denominator, the fraction represents more than 1 whole.'}, {'question': 'If you have 8 quarter-pieces (8 × 1/4) of a sandwich, how many whole sandwiches do you have?', 'options': ['1 sandwich', '2 sandwiches', '4 sandwiches', '8 sandwiches'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Every 4 fourths make 1 whole sandwich. 8/4 = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 whole sandwiches.', 'study_insight': 'Dividing numerator by denominator converts improper fractions to whole units.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Mixed Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Plotting Improper Fractions on the Number Line and Converting to Mixed Numbers', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">A <strong>mixed number</strong> combines a whole number and a proper fraction together (e.g., 2 1/2, read as <em>two and a half</em>).</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Conversion Steps: Improper Fraction to Mixed Number</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Divide the numerator by the denominator:<br>\n                • <strong>Quotient</strong> becomes the <em>Whole Number</em>.<br>\n                • <strong>Remainder</strong> becomes the new <em>Numerator</em>.<br>\n                • <strong>Divisor</strong> remains the <em>Denominator</em>.<br>\n                <em>Example:</em> Convert <strong>9/4</strong>:<br>\n                9 ÷ 4 = 2 with remainder 1 &rarr; <strong>9/4 = 2 1/4</strong> (two and one-fourth).</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 text-xs text-emerald-300">\n                <strong>Number Line Position:</strong> 2 1/4 lies between 2 and 3 on the number line, exactly one-quarter of the way past 2!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us connect improper fractions to the number line by learning about Mixed Numbers! A mixed number is a happy union of two mathematical parts: a whole number standing proudly on the left, and a proper fraction sitting beside it. Take the improper fraction nine-fourths. How do we write nine-fourths as a mixed number? We simply divide the numerator by the denominator! Divide nine by four. Four goes into nine two times, because four multiplied by two is eight. That quotient of two gives us two complete whole units! When you subtract eight from nine, you have a remainder of one. That remainder one becomes our new numerator, sitting over the original denominator four. So nine-fourths is written as two and one-fourth! Now imagine looking at a number line marked with zero, one, two, and three. Where does two and one-fourth sit? It sits past two, right in the interval between two and three, exactly one-quarter of the distance towards three! Converting between improper fractions and mixed numbers allows us to instantly visualize how big a quantity really is. Use the number line slider on your screen to jump between whole numbers and fractions, and watch the mixed number conversions happen dynamically!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the improper fraction 11/3 written as a mixed number?', 'options': ['3 1/3', '3 2/3', '2 2/3', '4 1/3'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '11 ÷ 3 = 3 with remainder 2. So 11/3 = 3 2/3 (three and two-thirds).', 'study_insight': '3 × 3 = 9, and 9 + 2 = 11, verifying that 3 2/3 = 11/3.'}, {'question': 'Between which two consecutive whole numbers does 17/5 lie on the number line?', 'options': ['Between 1 and 2', 'Between 2 and 3', 'Between 3 and 4', 'Between 4 and 5'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '17 ÷ 5 = 3 with remainder 2 (3 2/5). Since it is 3 plus 2/5, it lies between 3 and 4 on the number line.', 'study_insight': 'The whole number part of a mixed number identifies its preceding whole number on the line.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Benchmark One', 'subtitle': 'Using Whole Number 1 as a Benchmark to Compare Fractions', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When comparing two fractions, you do not always need to calculate common denominators! Often, you can simply compare both fractions to the <strong>benchmark of 1 (a whole)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-rose-400 block mb-1">Less Than 1 Whole</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">If numerator < denominator, the fraction is <strong>less than 1</strong>.<br>\n                    <em>Example:</em> <strong>7/8 < 1</strong> (needs 1/8 more to make a whole).</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Greater Than 1 Whole</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">If numerator > denominator, the fraction is <strong>greater than 1</strong>.<br>\n                    <em>Example:</em> <strong>8/6 > 1</strong> (equals 1 2/6, more than a whole).</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 text-xs text-amber-200">\n                <strong>Instant Comparison:</strong> Since 7/8 is less than 1, and 8/6 is greater than 1, we know immediately that <strong>7/8 < 8/6</strong> without any common denominator calculations!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Here is a brilliant mental math shortcut that real mathematicians use every day: benchmark comparisons! Often, when two fractions look complicated to compare, you do not need to do long calculations or find common denominators. All you need to do is compare each fraction to the benchmark number one! Ask yourself: is this fraction less than one whole, or greater than one whole? Consider this comparison: which is larger, seven-eighths or eight-sixths? Look at seven-eighths: the numerator seven is smaller than eight, which means you have seven slices out of an eight-slice pizza. You still need one more slice to make a whole, so seven-eighths is strictly less than one. Now look at eight-sixths: the numerator eight is greater than six, which means you have one whole pizza plus two extra slices! Eight-sixths is greater than one. Since seven-eighths is less than one, and eight-sixths is greater than one, seven-eighths is unquestionably smaller than eight-sixths! Just by comparing both fractions to the benchmark of one whole, we solved the problem in two seconds flat. Use the benchmark scale on your screen to test different pairs and watch how the benchmark one instantly separates smaller from larger fractions!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Compare 5/6 and 7/5 using 1 as a benchmark:', 'options': ['5/6 > 7/5', '5/6 < 7/5', '5/6 = 7/5', 'Cannot be compared'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '5/6 is less than 1 (numerator < denominator). 7/5 is greater than 1 (numerator > denominator). Therefore, 5/6 < 7/5.', 'study_insight': 'Any proper fraction is always smaller than any improper fraction.'}, {'question': 'Which of these fractions is strictly LESS than 1?', 'options': ['9/8', '4/3', '11/12', '6/5'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'In 11/12, numerator 11 is less than denominator 12, so 11/12 < 1. All other options have numerator > denominator.', 'study_insight': 'A proper fraction requires more parts to reach 1 whole.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Benchmark Half', 'subtitle': 'Using 1/2 as a Benchmark to Evaluate and Compare Fractions', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Another powerful benchmark is <strong>one-half (1/2)</strong>. To check whether a fraction is less than, equal to, or greater than 1/2, compare its numerator to <strong>half of its denominator</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-2 my-3 text-center">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-blue-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-400 block mb-1">Equal to 1/2</span>\n                    <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">Numerator = Half of Denominator<br>• <strong>3/6</strong> (3 is half of 6)<br>• <strong>4/8</strong> (4 is half of 8)</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Greater than 1/2</span>\n                    <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">Numerator > Half of Denominator<br>• <strong>5/8</strong> (5 > 4)<br>• <strong>7/10</strong> (7 > 5)</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-rose-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-rose-400 block mb-1">Less than 1/2</span>\n                    <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">Numerator < Half of Denominator<br>• <strong>2/6</strong> (2 < 3)<br>• <strong>3/8</strong> (3 < 4)</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Example: Compare 3/8 and 5/8. Half of 8 is 4. 3/8 is less than 1/2, while 5/8 is greater than 1/2. Thus, <strong>3/8 < 5/8</strong>!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Just as we used the number one as a benchmark, we can also use one-half as a powerful comparison landmark! How do you know if a fraction is equal to, greater than, or less than one-half? You simply look at its denominator, find half of that denominator, and compare it to the numerator! Let us practice. Take the fraction three-sixths. What is half of six? Half of six is three. Since the numerator is exactly three, three-sixths is equal to one-half! Now take five-eighths. What is half of eight? Half of eight is four. The numerator five is greater than four, so five-eighths is greater than one-half! Now look at two-sixths. The numerator two is less than three, so two-sixths is less than one-half. Now imagine you are asked to compare two-sixths and five-eighths. Instead of cross-multiplying, just glance at the benchmark of one-half: two-sixths is less than half, and five-eighths is more than half! Therefore, two-sixths is immediately smaller than five-eighths. Using benchmarks builds strong mathematical intuition and number sense. Test different fractions on the interactive benchmark meter on your screen to see them sort into less than half, exactly half, and greater than half!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following fractions is strictly GREATER than 1/2?', 'options': ['3/8', '4/10', '5/8', '2/6'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Half of 8 is 4. In 5/8, the numerator 5 is greater than 4, so 5/8 > 1/2. For all other options, numerator < half denominator.', 'study_insight': 'Check numerator against half of denominator: 5 > 4 means greater than 1/2.'}, {'question': 'Which fraction is exactly equal to 1/2?', 'options': ['5/12', '6/12', '7/12', '8/12'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Half of 12 is 6. So 6/12 = 1/2. (Dividing top and bottom by 6 gives 1/2).', 'study_insight': 'When the numerator is exactly half the denominator, the fraction simplifies to 1/2.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Ant Parade', 'subtitle': 'Word Problem: Calculating the Total Length of 16 Marching Ants', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In the <strong>Try This</strong> section of Chapter 2, students encounter a charming nature puzzle: <em>A line of 16 tiny ants is walking one after the other in a straight parade. If each ant measures 1/4 cm in length, what is the total length of the entire ant parade?</em></p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Mathematical Solution Steps</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Each ant = <strong>1/4 cm</strong>.<br>\n                Total number of ants = <strong>16</strong>.<br>\n                Total parade length = 16 × 1/4 cm = <strong>16/4 cm</strong>.<br>\n                Now divide 16 by 4:<br>\n                16 ÷ 4 = <strong>4 cm</strong>!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 text-xs text-emerald-300">\n                <strong>Number Line Hop:</strong> On a centimetre ruler, 4 ants make 1 cm (4 × 1/4 = 1). The next 4 ants make 2 cm, the next 4 make 3 cm, and the final 4 ants reach exactly <strong>4 cm</strong>!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us solve a delightful real-world puzzle from the Try This section of your textbook: The Ant Parade! Imagine looking down at your garden path and watching a disciplined line of sixteen tiny worker ants marching one right behind the other in a perfect straight parade. Each little ant measures exactly one-fourth of a centimetre in length. The question asks: what is the total length of the entire sixteen-ant parade from the head of the first ant to the tail of the last ant? Let us calculate this step by step! Every single ant contributes one-fourth of a centimetre. So with sixteen ants, the total length is sixteen multiplied by one-fourth, which gives the improper fraction sixteen over four centimetres. Now, what is sixteen divided by four? Sixteen divided by four is exactly four! So the entire parade measures four centimetres long! You can also visualize this using groups of four: four ants join together to make one full centimetre. The next four ants make a second centimetre. The next four make a third centimetre. And the last four ants complete the fourth centimetre! Sixteen fourths equals four whole centimetres. Watch the interactive ant parade march across the centimetre ruler on your screen, and see how repeated addition of fractions creates whole centimetres!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 12 ants of length 1/4 cm each walk in a single line, what is the total length of the ant line?', 'options': ['2 cm', '3 cm', '4 cm', '12 cm'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Total length = 12 × 1/4 cm = 12/4 cm = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 cm.', 'study_insight': '12 fourths equals 3 whole centimetres.'}, {'question': 'How many ants measuring 1/4 cm each are needed to form a line measuring exactly 5 cm?', 'options': ['10 ants', '15 ants', '20 ants', '25 ants'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Each centimetre requires 4 ants. For 5 cm, you need 5 × 4 = 20 ants.', 'study_insight': '5 ÷ (1/4) = 5 × 4 = 20 ants.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Family Riddle', 'subtitle': 'Fun with Relations: Deductive Logic and Relationship Riddles', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In the <strong>Fun with Relations</strong> section, the textbook presents a classic Indian family logic riddle: <em>“If the only brother of your father\'s sister had a child, what would be the child\'s relationship with you?”</em></p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-2">Step-by-Step Logical Deduction</span>\n                <ol class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-1.5 list-decimal list-inside">\n                    <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> “Your father\'s sister” is your paternal aunt (often called <em>Bua</em> in Hindi).</li>\n                    <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Who is the “<em>only brother</em>” of your father\'s sister? Since your father is her brother, and she has only <em>one</em> brother, that only brother is <strong>your father himself</strong>!</li>\n                    <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Now, the riddle asks about “the child of your father”. Who is your father\'s child? <strong>You yourself, or your brother/sister (sibling)!</strong></li>\n                </ol>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Just like simplifying a fraction step-by-step to its lowest terms, analyzing relationship riddles step-by-step leads directly to an elegant, logical answer.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "To sharpen our logical reasoning, the textbook includes a wonderful family riddle in the Fun with Relations section! Listen carefully to the clues: If the only brother of your father's sister had a child, what would be the child's relationship with you? Let us break this puzzle down like true mathematical detectives. First, think about your father's sister. In Indian families, your father's sister is your aunt, affectionately called Bua in Hindi. Next, who is the only brother of your father's sister? Since she has only one brother, and your father is her brother, that only brother can be none other than your very own father! Now the riddle becomes delightfully simple: who is the child of your father? Your father's child is either you yourself, or your brother, or your sister! That means the child is your sibling, or you! In mathematics, whether we are simplifying a complex fraction or solving a word puzzle, breaking a problem into clear, deductive steps always turns confusion into clarity. Use the interactive family tree diagram on your screen to trace the clues and reveal the secret connection!", 'checkpoints': [{'question': "In the family riddle, who is the 'only brother of your father's sister'?", 'options': ['Your uncle (Chacha)', 'Your grandfather', 'Your father', 'Your cousin'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': "Your father's sister is your aunt. If she has only one brother, that brother must be your father.", 'study_insight': 'Deductive logic breaks down complex statements one phrase at a time.'}, {'question': 'What is the relationship between you and a child of your father?', 'options': ['Cousin', 'Sibling (or yourself)', 'Uncle', 'Nephew'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': "Your father's children are your brothers, your sisters, or yourself (siblings).", 'study_insight': 'Tracing kinship links mirrors simplifying mathematical expressions.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Fraction Lab', 'subtitle': 'Interactive Synthesis: Equivalence, Benchmarks, and Mixed Numbers', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Welcome to the complete <strong>Fraction Laboratory</strong>! Here we synthesize all the key concepts mastered in Chapter 2 into an interactive mathematical workshop.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-2 my-3 text-center text-xs font-mono">\n                <div class="p-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-amber-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-[10px] text-amber-400 font-bold block">Equal Wholes</span>\n                    <span class="text-white">Fair Baseline</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-blue-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-[10px] text-blue-400 font-bold block">Equivalence</span>\n                    <span class="text-white">1/3 = 2/6 = 4/12</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-[10px] text-emerald-400 font-bold block">Benchmarks</span>\n                    <span class="text-white">Compare to 1/2 & 1</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-purple-500/30">\n                    <span class="text-[10px] text-purple-400 font-bold block">Mixed Numbers</span>\n                    <span class="text-white">7/3 = 2 1/3</span>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">You are now equipped with full mastery of fractions—from sharing parathas and shading grids to finding equivalent names, using benchmarks, and converting improper fractions!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Congratulations young mathematicians! You have completed your exploration of all the core concepts in Chapter Two on Fractions. Let us take a moment to look back at the grand mathematical toolkit you now possess. First, you learned that fractions can only be fairly compared when the original reference wholes are identical. Second, you discovered how to visualize fractional parts by shading square grids and fitting colorful fraction strips together. Third, you mastered equivalent fractions: different names like one-third, two-sixths, and four-twelfths that describe the exact same shaded region. Fourth, you uncovered the golden rules of comparison: when denominators are equal, larger numerators win; and when numerators are equal, smaller denominators win! Fifth, you journeyed beyond one whole into improper fractions and mixed numbers like five-halves and two and a half. And finally, you learned to use benchmarks like one-half and one whole to evaluate fractions in seconds, and applied your skills to ant parades and family riddles. Now, you are ready to tackle the four Mastery Challenges on the upcoming slides! Experiment with the all-in-one Fraction Lab on your screen and celebrate your mathematical success!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following statements summarizes the core rule of equivalent fractions?', 'options': ['They have the same numbers and different values', 'They have different numbers but represent the exact same value', 'They can only have 1 as their numerator', 'They are always greater than 1'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Equivalent fractions (like 1/2, 2/4, 4/8) have different numerators and denominators but equal mathematical value.', 'study_insight': 'Equivalence means equal value despite different numerical representations.'}, {'question': 'If you compare 4/7 and 5/9 using benchmarks, which fraction is greater than 1/2?', 'options': ['4/7 is greater than 1/2', '5/9 is greater than 1/2', 'Both 4/7 and 5/9 are greater than 1/2', 'Neither is greater than 1/2'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Half of 7 is 3.5; 4 > 3.5, so 4/7 > 1/2. Half of 9 is 4.5; 5 > 4.5, so 5/9 > 1/2 as well! Both are greater than 1/2.', 'study_insight': 'Checking both numerators against half their denominators quickly validates both are above 1/2.'}]}]

QUIZ_QUESTIONS = [{'id': 1, 'question': 'Why is 1/2 of a small paratha NOT equal to 1/2 of a large paratha?', 'options': ['Because half always equals one quarter', 'Because the wholes from which the halves are taken have different sizes', 'Because parathas cannot be cut into equal halves', 'Because fractions only apply to rectangular shapes'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'To compare fractions accurately, the wholes must be identical. Half of a large whole contains more area than half of a small whole.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. A fraction represents a relative portion of a specific whole.<br>2. If Whole A has an area of 50 sq cm, 1/2 of A = 25 sq cm.<br>3. If Whole B has an area of 100 sq cm, 1/2 of B = 50 sq cm.<br>4. Even though both are '1/2', 50 sq cm > 25 sq cm. Wholes must be identical to compare fractions directly."}, {'id': 2, 'question': 'A square grid contains 16 identical smaller squares. How many squares represent 1/4 of the grid?', 'options': ['2 squares', '4 squares', '8 squares', '6 squares'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'To find 1/4 of 16 squares, divide 16 by 4: 16 ÷ 4 = 4 squares.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Total squares in grid = 16.<br>2. Required fraction = 1/4.<br>3. 16 × (1/4) = 16 ÷ 4 = <strong>4 squares</strong>.'}, {'id': 3, 'question': 'In Grid B with 24 equal squares, 1/6 is shaded in blue. How many blue squares are there?', 'options': ['3 squares', '4 squares', '6 squares', '8 squares'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '1/6 of 24 = 24 ÷ 6 = 4 squares.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Total squares = 24.<br>2. Fraction shaded = 1/6.<br>3. 24 ÷ 6 = <strong>4 squares</strong>.'}, {'id': 4, 'question': 'In Grid C with 36 equal squares, 3 squares are shaded yellow. What fraction of Grid C is shaded?', 'options': ['1/6', '1/8', '1/12', '1/9'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '3 out of 36 is 3/36. Dividing numerator and denominator by 3 gives 1/12.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Shaded squares = 3; Total squares = 36.<br>2. Fraction = 3/36.<br>3. Divide top and bottom by 3: (3 ÷ 3) / (36 ÷ 3) = <strong>1/12</strong>.'}, {'id': 5, 'question': 'How many 1/8 pieces from the fraction kit equal one 1/2 piece?', 'options': ['2 pieces', '4 pieces', '6 pieces', '8 pieces'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '4/8 = 1/2. Exactly four 1/8 pieces match the length of one 1/2 piece.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. We want: ? × 1/8 = 1/2.<br>2. Convert 1/2 to eighths: (1 × 4) / (2 × 4) = 4/8.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>4 pieces</strong> of 1/8 are needed.'}, {'id': 6, 'question': 'How many 1/4 pieces are needed to make one complete whole strip?', 'options': ['2 pieces', '3 pieces', '4 pieces', '8 pieces'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '4/4 = 1 Whole. Four 1/4 pieces make 1 whole strip.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Whole = 1.<br>2. 1 ÷ (1/4) = 1 × 4 = <strong>4 pieces</strong>.'}, {'id': 7, 'question': 'If you combine two 1/4 pieces and four 1/8 pieces, what total fraction of the strip do you have?', 'options': ['1/2', '3/4', '1 Whole', '5/8'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Two 1/4 pieces = 2/4 = 1/2. Four 1/8 pieces = 4/8 = 1/2. 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 Whole!', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Two 1/4 pieces = 2/4 = 1/2.<br>2. Four 1/8 pieces = 4/8 = 1/2.<br>3. 1/2 + 1/2 = <strong>1 Whole</strong>.'}, {'id': 8, 'question': 'Which piece relationship is TRUE in the fraction kit?', 'options': ['Two 1/8 pieces = 1/2', 'Two 1/8 pieces = 1/4', 'Two 1/4 pieces = 1 Whole', 'Four 1/8 pieces = 1/4'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '2/8 simplifies to 1/4 by dividing numerator and denominator by 2.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 1/8 + 1/8 = 2/8.<br>2. (2 ÷ 2) / (8 ÷ 2) = <strong>1/4</strong>.<br>3. Thus, two 1/8 pieces equal 1/4.'}, {'id': 9, 'question': 'How many 1/16 pieces equal one 1/4 piece?', 'options': ['2 pieces', '4 pieces', '8 pieces', '16 pieces'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '4/16 = 1/4. Four 1/16 pieces match the length of one 1/4 piece.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Convert 1/4 to sixteenths: (1 × 4) / (4 × 4) = 4/16.<br>2. So exactly <strong>4 pieces</strong> of 1/16 equal 1/4.'}, {'id': 10, 'question': "In a fraction a/b, what does the denominator 'b' represent?", 'options': ['The number of parts we are taking', 'The total number of equal parts into which the whole is divided', 'The size of the whole in kilograms', 'The number of people sharing the food'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The denominator tells us the total number of equal parts that constitute one whole unit.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. In any fraction a/b:<br>• <strong>b (Denominator)</strong> = total equal parts dividing the whole.<br>• <strong>a (Numerator)</strong> = number of equal parts taken or considered.'}, {'id': 11, 'question': 'Which combination of fraction pieces equals exactly 1 Whole?', 'options': ['1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8', '1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4', '1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4', '1/3 + 1/3'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2 + 2/4 = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 Whole.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 1/4 + 1/4 = 2/4 = 1/2.<br>2. 1/2 + 1/2 = 2/2 = <strong>1 Whole</strong>.'}, {'id': 12, 'question': 'If you have 1/3 and 2/6, how much more do you need to complete 1 Whole?', 'options': ['1/2', '1/3', '1/6', '1/4'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '2/6 = 1/3. So 1/3 + 2/6 = 1/3 + 1/3 = 2/3. To reach 3/3 (1 Whole), you need 1/3 more.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Simplify 2/6 = 1/3.<br>2. Current sum = 1/3 + 1/3 = 2/3.<br>3. Needed = 1 - 2/3 = 3/3 - 2/3 = <strong>1/3</strong>.'}, {'id': 13, 'question': 'Which fraction is NOT equivalent to 1/2?', 'options': ['2/4', '3/6', '4/8', '5/12'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'In 5/12, half of 12 is 6, not 5. So 5/12 is less than 1/2, not equivalent to 1/2.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 2/4 = 1/2 (divide by 2).<br>2. 3/6 = 1/2 (divide by 3).<br>3. 4/8 = 1/2 (divide by 4).<br>4. 5/12 ≠ 1/2 (since 6/12 = 1/2). So <strong>5/12</strong> is not equivalent to 1/2.'}, {'id': 14, 'question': 'Find the missing number in: 2/5 = ?/15', 'options': ['4', '5', '6', '8'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '5 × 3 = 15. So 2 × 3 = 6. 2/5 = 6/15.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Compare denominators: 15 ÷ 5 = 3 (scale factor = 3).<br>2. Multiply numerator by 3: 2 × 3 = <strong>6</strong>.<br>3. Therefore, 2/5 = 6/15.'}, {'id': 15, 'question': 'Find the missing denominator: 3/4 = 12/?', 'options': ['14', '15', '16', '18'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '3 × 4 = 12. So 4 × 4 = 16. 3/4 = 12/16.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Compare numerators: 12 ÷ 3 = 4 (scale factor = 4).<br>2. Multiply denominator by 4: 4 × 4 = <strong>16</strong>.<br>3. Therefore, 3/4 = 12/16.'}, {'id': 16, 'question': 'What is the simplest (lowest terms) form of 8/12?', 'options': ['4/6', '2/3', '1/2', '3/4'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Divide both 8 and 12 by their greatest common factor, 4: 8 ÷ 4 = 2, and 12 ÷ 4 = 3. The simplest form is 2/3.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Factors of 8: 1, 2, 4, 8.<br>2. Factors of 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12.<br>3. Highest common factor = 4.<br>4. (8 ÷ 4) / (12 ÷ 4) = <strong>2/3</strong>.'}, {'id': 17, 'question': 'If you multiply the numerator and denominator of 4/7 by 5, what equivalent fraction do you get?', 'options': ['20/35', '9/12', '20/28', '16/35'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': '(4 × 5) / (7 × 5) = 20/35.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Numerator: 4 × 5 = 20.<br>2. Denominator: 7 × 5 = 35.<br>3. Result = <strong>20/35</strong>.'}, {'id': 18, 'question': 'Which two fractions are equivalent?', 'options': ['2/3 and 4/9', '3/5 and 6/10', '1/4 and 2/6', '3/4 and 6/12'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '3/5 = (3 × 2) / (5 × 2) = 6/10. Cross multiplication: 3 × 10 = 30 and 5 × 6 = 30.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. For 3/5 and 6/10: 3 × 2 = 6, and 5 × 2 = 10.<br>2. Cross check: 3 × 10 = 30; 5 × 6 = 30 (Equal!).<br>3. So <strong>3/5 and 6/10</strong> are equivalent.'}, {'id': 19, 'question': 'Find the missing value: 5/8 = 25/?', 'options': ['35', '40', '45', '30'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '5 × 5 = 25. So 8 × 5 = 40. 5/8 = 25/40.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 25 ÷ 5 = 5.<br>2. 8 × 5 = <strong>40</strong>.<br>3. Therefore, 5/8 = 25/40.'}, {'id': 20, 'question': 'What happens to the value of a fraction if you multiply both numerator and denominator by 10?', 'options': ['The value becomes 10 times larger', 'The value becomes 10 times smaller', 'The value stays exactly the same', 'The fraction becomes improper'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Multiplying both numerator and denominator by the same non-zero number produces an equivalent fraction with the exact same value.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Multiplying top and bottom by 10 is equivalent to multiplying by 10/10, which equals 1.<br>2. Any number multiplied by 1 <strong>stays exactly the same</strong> in value.'}, {'id': 21, 'question': 'Compare: 4/9 ___ 7/9', 'options': ['>', '<', '=', 'Cannot be compared'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Since denominators are identical (9), compare numerators: 4 < 7. So 4/9 < 7/9.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Denominators match: both are 9.<br>2. Compare numerators: 4 < 7.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>4/9 < 7/9</strong>.'}, {'id': 22, 'question': 'Compare: 5/7 ___ 5/9', 'options': ['>', '<', '=', 'None of these'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'When numerators match, the smaller denominator has larger pieces. Since 7 < 9, sevenths are bigger than ninths: 5/7 > 5/9.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Numerators match: both are 5.<br>2. Compare denominators: 7 < 9.<br>3. Smaller denominator means larger piece size: 1/7 > 1/9.<br>4. Therefore, <strong>5/7 > 5/9</strong>.'}, {'id': 23, 'question': 'Which of these fractions is the LARGEST?', 'options': ['2/9', '2/7', '2/5', '2/3'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'All numerators are 2. The smallest denominator (3) produces the largest individual pieces. So 2/3 is the largest.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. All numerators = 2.<br>2. Smallest denominator = 3 (thirds are larger than fifths, sevenths, or ninths).<br>3. Therefore, <strong>2/3</strong> is the largest fraction.'}, {'id': 24, 'question': 'Which of these fractions is the SMALLEST?', 'options': ['5/6', '3/6', '1/6', '4/6'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'All denominators are 6. The smallest numerator (1) represents the fewest slices: 1/6 is the smallest.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. All denominators = 6.<br>2. Compare numerators: 1 < 3 < 4 < 5.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>1/6</strong> is the smallest.'}, {'id': 25, 'question': 'Which of the following is an improper fraction (greater than 1)?', 'options': ['5/6', '3/4', '8/5', '7/9'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'In 8/5, the numerator 8 is greater than the denominator 5, making it an improper fraction greater than 1.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. A fraction is improper if numerator ≥ denominator.<br>2. For 8/5: 8 > 5.<br>3. 8/5 = 1 3/5, which is <strong>greater than 1</strong>.'}, {'id': 26, 'question': 'If you have 7 half-parathas (7 × 1/2), how many whole parathas and leftover halves do you have?', 'options': ['2 whole parathas and 1 half', '3 whole parathas and 1 half', '4 whole parathas', '3 whole parathas and no halves'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '7 ÷ 2 = 3 with remainder 1. So 7 halves = 3 whole parathas and 1/2 paratha.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Every 2 halves make 1 whole.<br>2. 7 ÷ 2 = 3 with remainder 1.<br>3. So 7/2 = <strong>3 wholes and 1/2</strong> (3 1/2).'}, {'id': 27, 'question': 'What is 10/4 simplified and expressed as a mixed number?', 'options': ['2 1/4', '2 1/2', '2 3/4', '3 1/4'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '10 ÷ 4 = 2 with remainder 2, which gives 2 2/4. Simplifying 2/4 gives 2 1/2.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 10 ÷ 4 = 2 with remainder 2 &rarr; 2 2/4.<br>2. Simplify 2/4 by dividing by 2 &rarr; 1/2.<br>3. Result = <strong>2 1/2</strong>.'}, {'id': 28, 'question': 'Which symbol makes this statement true: 7/8 ___ 7/10?', 'options': ['>', '<', '=', '+'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Same numerator (7). Denominator 8 < 10, so eighths are larger than tenths: 7/8 > 7/10.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Numerators are equal: 7 = 7.<br>2. Denominators: 8 < 10 &rarr; 1/8 > 1/10.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>7/8 > 7/10</strong>.'}, {'id': 29, 'question': 'How many whole units are in 15/5?', 'options': ['2', '3', '4', '5'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '15 ÷ 5 = 3 exactly, with zero remainder. So 15/5 = 3 whole units.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 15 ÷ 5 = <strong>3</strong>.<br>2. There is no fractional remainder; 15/5 is equal to the whole number 3.'}, {'id': 30, 'question': 'If Kabir eats 3/8 of a pizza and Ananya eats 5/8 of the same pizza, who ate more?', 'options': ['Kabir ate more', 'Ananya ate more', 'Both ate the same amount', 'Cannot be determined'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '5/8 > 3/8 because denominators are identical and 5 > 3. Ananya ate more.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Compare 5/8 and 3/8.<br>2. 5/8 > 3/8.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>Ananya ate more</strong>.'}, {'id': 31, 'question': 'Convert the improper fraction 13/4 to a mixed number:', 'options': ['3 1/4', '3 3/4', '2 3/4', '4 1/4'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': '13 ÷ 4 = 3 with remainder 1. So 13/4 = 3 1/4.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Divide 13 by 4: 13 ÷ 4 = 3 (whole number).<br>2. Remainder = 13 - (4 × 3) = 1.<br>3. Mixed number = <strong>3 1/4</strong>.'}, {'id': 32, 'question': 'Convert the mixed number 2 3/5 back to an improper fraction:', 'options': ['11/5', '13/5', '15/5', '10/5'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '(2 × 5) + 3 = 10 + 3 = 13. So 2 3/5 = 13/5.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Multiply whole number by denominator: 2 × 5 = 10.<br>2. Add numerator: 10 + 3 = 13.<br>3. Keep denominator: <strong>13/5</strong>.'}, {'id': 33, 'question': 'Compare 6/7 and 8/5 using 1 as a benchmark:', 'options': ['6/7 > 8/5', '6/7 < 8/5', '6/7 = 8/5', 'Cannot be compared'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '6/7 < 1 (proper fraction). 8/5 > 1 (improper fraction). Therefore, 6/7 < 8/5.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Benchmark check against 1:<br>• 6/7 < 1 (since 6 < 7).<br>• 8/5 > 1 (since 8 > 5).<br>2. Since 6/7 < 1 < 8/5, we know <strong>6/7 < 8/5</strong>.'}, {'id': 34, 'question': 'Which of these fractions is strictly LESS than 1/2?', 'options': ['4/8', '5/8', '3/8', '7/12'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Half of 8 is 4. In 3/8, the numerator 3 is less than 4, so 3/8 < 1/2.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Benchmark 1/2 of 8 = 4/8.<br>2. Comparing: 3/8 < 4/8.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>3/8</strong> is less than 1/2.'}, {'id': 35, 'question': 'Which of these fractions is strictly GREATER than 1/2?', 'options': ['4/10', '5/10', '7/10', '3/10'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Half of 10 is 5. In 7/10, the numerator 7 is greater than 5, so 7/10 > 1/2.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Benchmark 1/2 of 10 = 5/10.<br>2. 7/10 > 5/10.<br>3. Therefore, <strong>7/10</strong> is greater than 1/2.'}, {'id': 36, 'question': 'A line of 16 ants marches in a line. Each ant is 1/4 cm long. What is the total length of the line?', 'options': ['2 cm', '3 cm', '4 cm', '8 cm'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Total length = 16 × 1/4 = 16/4 = 4 cm.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Length of 1 ant = 1/4 cm.<br>2. Number of ants = 16.<br>3. Total length = 16 × 1/4 = 16/4 = <strong>4 cm</strong>.'}, {'id': 37, 'question': 'If 20 ants each measure 1/4 cm, what is the total length of the parade?', 'options': ['4 cm', '5 cm', '6 cm', '10 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '20 × 1/4 = 20/4 = 5 cm.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 20 × (1/4) = 20/4 cm.<br>2. 20 ÷ 4 = <strong>5 cm</strong>.'}, {'id': 38, 'question': "In the Family Riddle: 'If the only brother of your father's sister had a child, what would be the child's relationship with you?'", 'options': ['Uncle', 'Cousin', 'Your sibling (or yourself)', 'Grandfather'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': "Father's sister is your aunt. Her ONLY brother is your father. A child of your father is your sibling (or yourself).", 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Father's sister = Aunt (Bua).<br>2. Only brother of Aunt = Your Father.<br>3. Child of your Father = <strong>Your sibling (brother/sister) or yourself</strong>."}, {'id': 39, 'question': 'Between which two numbers on a number line does 9/2 lie?', 'options': ['Between 3 and 4', 'Between 4 and 5', 'Between 5 and 6', 'Between 2 and 3'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '9 ÷ 2 = 4 1/2. 4 1/2 lies exactly halfway between 4 and 5.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. 9 ÷ 2 = 4 with remainder 1 &rarr; 4 1/2.<br>2. 4 1/2 is greater than 4 and less than 5.<br>3. So it lies <strong>between 4 and 5</strong>.'}, {'id': 40, 'question': 'Which fraction is equivalent to 3/4 AND has a denominator of 20?', 'options': ['12/20', '15/20', '16/20', '18/20'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'From 4 to 20, multiply by 5. (3 × 5) / (4 × 5) = 15/20.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Solution:</strong><br>1. Target denominator = 20.<br>2. Scale factor: 20 ÷ 4 = 5.<br>3. Multiply numerator by 5: 3 × 5 = 15.<br>4. Result = <strong>15/20</strong>.'}]
